====== gfg Find nth root of m =======

Step-by-step algorithm to find the nth root of a number m using Binary Search:

Step 1: If m is less than or equal to 0, return m directly.

Step 2: Initialize two variables:

low = 1

high = m

Step 3: Start a loop while low is less than or equal to high.

Step 4: Find the middle value:

mid = low + (high - low) / 2

Step 5: Calculate mid raised to the power n.

Sub-step 5a: Initialize power = 1

Sub-step 5b: Multiply power by mid, n times

Sub-step 5c: If at any point power becomes greater than m, stop early

Step 6: Compare power with m.

Sub-step 6a: If power is equal to m, return mid
(We found the exact nth root)

Sub-step 6b: If power is greater than m,
set high = mid - 1
(Search in the left half)

Sub-step 6c: If power is less than m,
set low = mid + 1
(Search in the right half)

Step 7: Repeat the process until low becomes greater than high.

Step 8: If no exact nth root is found, return -1.

Time Complexity:
Binary search runs in O(log m)
For each mid, we compute power in O(n)
Total Time Complexity = O(n × log m)

Space Complexity:
Only a few variables are used.
Space Complexity = O(1)


            ------------------> Code <--------------------

class Solution {
    public int nthRoot(int n, int m) {
        if(m<=0)return m;
        int low=1, high=m;
        while(low<=high){
            int mid=low+(high-low)/2;
            long power=1;
            
            for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
                power*=mid;
                if(power>m)break;
            }
            
            if(power==m){
                return mid;
            }else if(power>m){
                high=mid-1;
            }else{
                low=mid+1;
            }
            
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
